{"ClientSettings":{"Pn":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"P"},"Sc":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"H"},"Qz":{"Cn":1,"St":0,"Qs":0,"Prod":"T"},"Ap":true,"Mute":true,"Lad":"2026-10-16T00:00:00Z","Iotd":0,"Gwb":0,"Tns":0,"Dft":null,"Mvs":0,"Flt":0,"Imp":1,"Tobn":0},"MediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"People have been coming to Niagara Falls, on the Canada-US border, for centuries to admire the view. A few, however, decided admiring it from a safe distance was overrated. In 1901, Annie Edson Taylor climbed into a barrel and became the first recorded person to survive going over the Falls. Others followed with their own stunts, although such attempts are now illegal. The Falls have also inspired artists, filmmakers and musicians, with Hollywood giving them a starring role in 'Niagara' (1953), featuring Marilyn Monroe and Joseph Cotten.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.NiagaraOnt_ROW1418204353_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.NiagaraOnt_ROW1418204353_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Horseshoe Falls illuminated at night in Niagara Falls, Ontario, Canada","Copyright":"© Jianmei Wang/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261015_NiagaraOnt\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Horseshoe+Falls+Niagara+Falls&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261015_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261015_NiagaraOnt"},"Ssd":"20261015","Name":"NiagaraOnt","Market":"ROW","Hash":"1418204353","FullDateString":"15 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"How do we know what Earth looked like long before humans appeared? Fossils provide many clues. Palaeontological discoveries help scientists reconstruct past environments, trace the evolution of life and learn about creatures that vanished millions of years ago. From dinosaurs to tiny marine organisms, these remnants of the past preserve snapshots of ancient worlds that no longer exist.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.AmmoniteFossils_ROW1149504413_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.AmmoniteFossils_ROW1149504413_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Ammonite fossils from the Jurassic Period","Copyright":"© J Nemchinova/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261014_AmmoniteFossils\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Ammonite+fossils&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261014_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261014_AmmoniteFossils"},"Ssd":"20261014","Name":"AmmoniteFossils","Market":"ROW","Hash":"1149504413","FullDateString":"14 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Water seems to be everywhere at Plitvice Lakes National Park in Croatia. Sixteen terraced lakes are linked by a network of waterfalls, streams and cascades that wind through forested limestone canyons. The constantly changing colours of the water, ranging from emerald green to deep blue, are shaped by minerals, sunlight and ever-changing reflections. Established in 1949, Plitvice Lakes is Croatia's oldest and largest national park. The landscape is famous for its tufa barriers, natural limestone formations created as mineral-rich water deposits calcium carbonate over centuries. These barriers continue to grow and change, gradually transforming the park's waterfalls and lakes.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.PlitviceAutumn_ROW0890292044_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.PlitviceAutumn_ROW0890292044_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Plitvice Lakes National Park, Croatia","Copyright":"© Jan Wlodarczyk/Alamy","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261013_PlitviceAutumn\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Plitvice+Lakes+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261013_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261013_PlitviceAutumn"},"Ssd":"20261013","Name":"PlitviceAutumn","Market":"ROW","Hash":"0890292044","FullDateString":"13 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"What do you get when a railway, a lifeboat station and a seaside day out meet at the water's edge? Mumbles Pier. Stretching 255 metres into Swansea Bay, this Victorian landmark opened on 10 May 1898 and has been part of the Welsh coast ever since.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.MumblesSwansea2026_ROW0047867144_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.MumblesSwansea2026_ROW0047867144_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Mumbles Pier, Swansea, Wales","Copyright":"© Helen Hotson/Shutterstock","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261012_MumblesSwansea2026\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Mumbles+Pier+Wales&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261012_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261012_MumblesSwansea2026"},"Ssd":"20261012","Name":"MumblesSwansea2026","Market":"ROW","Hash":"0047867144","FullDateString":"12 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Surrounded by a patchwork of red, orange and gold, Glade Creek Grist Mill stands above the rocky waters of Glade Creek in West Virginia's Babcock State Park, United States. Although it looks like a relic from the pioneer era, the mill is actually a carefully crafted tribute to Appalachia's milling heritage.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.BabcockSP_ROW9110416835_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.BabcockSP_ROW9110416835_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Glade Creek Grist Mill, Babcock State Park, West Virginia, United States","Copyright":"© dszc/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261011_BabcockSP\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Babcock+State+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261011_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261011_BabcockSP"},"Ssd":"20261011","Name":"BabcockSP","Market":"ROW","Hash":"9110416835","FullDateString":"11 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Today's image has a little airborne drama: double-crested cormorants are cruising above the waves of Monterey Bay, California, United States. Widespread across North America, these adaptable seabirds may migrate or remain year-round, depending on the population. Along California's coast, bays, wetlands and estuaries provide important habitat for birds travelling the Pacific Flyway.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.CormorantsFlight_ROW8571720617_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.CormorantsFlight_ROW8571720617_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Double-crested cormorants over Monterey Bay, California, United States","Copyright":"© Hiroya Minakuchi/Minden Pictures","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261010_CormorantsFlight\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Double+crested+cormorant&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261010_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261010_CormorantsFlight"},"Ssd":"20261010","Name":"CormorantsFlight","Market":"ROW","Hash":"8571720617","FullDateString":"10 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Just off the coast of Corsica, France, the Sanguinaires Islands rise from the Mediterranean as a small archipelago of four rugged, rocky islands. Viewed from a historic watchtower, they sit at the entrance to the Gulf of Ajaccio. Steep cliffs, centuries-old landmarks and sweeping sea views have drawn visitors to the area for generations. Though the farthest island lies only about 1.9 kilometres from shore, the archipelago feels surprisingly remote, surrounded by open water and shaped by wind, waves and salt spray.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.IlesSanguinaires_ROW8228238206_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.IlesSanguinaires_ROW8228238206_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"View of the Sanguinaires Islands from Corsica, France","Copyright":"© Francesco Riccardo Iacomino/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261009_IlesSanguinaires\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Sanguinaires+Islands+Corsica&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261009_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261009_IlesSanguinaires"},"Ssd":"20261009","Name":"IlesSanguinaires","Market":"ROW","Hash":"8228238206","FullDateString":"9 Oct 2026"}],"PreloadMediaContents":[{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Few places in South Korea capture the magic of autumn quite like Naejangsan National Park, where mountainsides glow in brilliant shades of scarlet, orange, yellow and gold. Located in the country's southwest, the park is particularly famous for its vivid maple colours, making it one of Korea's premier destinations for autumn scenery. Its name, meaning 'many secrets in the mountains,' reflects the varied landscapes found throughout the region.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.NaejangsanNP_ROW0481262720_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.NaejangsanNP_ROW0481262720_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Autumn in Naejangsan National Park, South Korea","Copyright":"© Twenty47studio/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261022_NaejangsanNP\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Naejangsan+National+Park&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261022_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261022_NaejangsanNP"},"Ssd":"20261022","Name":"NaejangsanNP","Market":"ROW","Hash":"0481262720","FullDateString":"22 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Bridging the gap between old and new, Nguyen Hoang Bridge gives Hue's skyline a fresh connection. Spanning the Perfume River in Vietnam, the modern steel-arch landmark opened to traffic on March 26, 2025. Its design, called 'The Crane Facing Thien Mu,' draws inspiration from a local legend and nearby Thien Mu Pagoda.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.VietnamBridge_ROW0147548772_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.VietnamBridge_ROW0147548772_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Nguyen Hoang Bridge over the Perfume River, Hue, Vietnam","Copyright":"© VU PHAM VAN/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261021_VietnamBridge\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Hue+Vietnam&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261021_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261021_VietnamBridge"},"Ssd":"20261021","Name":"VietnamBridge","Market":"ROW","Hash":"0147548772","FullDateString":"21 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Slow and steady wins the race—and the brown-throated sloth seems perfectly happy to skip the race altogether. This three-toed mammal has a different approach to life: conserve energy, hang on and take your time.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.BrownSloth_ROW9869731535_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.BrownSloth_ROW9869731535_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Brown-throated sloth, Puerto Viejo de Talamanca, Costa Rica","Copyright":"© Suzi Eszterhas/Minden Pictures","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261020_BrownSloth\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Brown+throated+sloth&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261020_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261020_BrownSloth"},"Ssd":"20261020","Name":"BrownSloth","Market":"ROW","Hash":"9869731535","FullDateString":"20 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"What if a city's busiest street had no road? In Venice, Italy, traffic flows along the Grand Canal, a 3.8-kilometre waterway that winds through the city in a giant reverse S-shape. Known to locals as the Canalazzo, it is Venice's main water-traffic corridor, carrying vaporetti, water taxis, gondolas, police boats and other vessels. The canal follows the course of an ancient river that once flowed into the Venetian Lagoon.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.VeniceSunrise_ROW9643744702_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.VeniceSunrise_ROW9643744702_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Grand Canal in Venice, Italy","Copyright":"© Juan Romero/Cavan Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261019_VeniceSunrise\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Grand+Canal+Venice&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261019_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261019_VeniceSunrise"},"Ssd":"20261019","Name":"VeniceSunrise","Market":"ROW","Hash":"9643744702","FullDateString":"19 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"If parks could keep birthday diaries, Eryri National Park (Snowdonia) in Wales would have quite the story to tell. The park turns 75 today, marking the anniversary of its designation in 1951. Eryri stretches across 2,130 square kilometres, with nine mountain ranges, 119 kilometres of coastline and habitats ranging from peatlands and native woodlands to upland landscapes. At more than 1,050 metres, Yr Wyddfa is Eryri's highest point. Nearby, the Carneddau and Glyderau add their own peaks, while Cadair Idris rises in the south. These protected landscapes provide habitats for wildlife and include 17 National Nature Reserves and 56 Sites of Special Scientific Interest.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.DolbadarnWales_ROW9422076532_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.DolbadarnWales_ROW9422076532_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Dolbadarn Castle, Llanberis, Eryri National Park (Snowdonia), Wales","Copyright":"© joe daniel price/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261018_DolbadarnWales\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Eryri+National+Park+Wales&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261018_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261018_DolbadarnWales"},"Ssd":"20261018","Name":"DolbadarnWales","Market":"ROW","Hash":"9422076532","FullDateString":"18 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Perugia has a walkway with a story beneath every step: its medieval aqueduct. Construction began in 1254, and by around 1280, the aqueduct was carrying water from Monte Pacciano to the Fontana Maggiore in the city centre. The clever bit? Engineers found a way to make the water flow uphill. Using a pressurised conduit, the system overcame the city's steep terrain—an impressive feat of medieval engineering.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.PerugiaAqueduct_ROW9255897123_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.PerugiaAqueduct_ROW9255897123_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Medieval Aqueduct, Perugia, Italy","Copyright":"© Marco Saracco/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261017_PerugiaAqueduct\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Medieval+Aqueduct+Perugia&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261017_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\"","TriviaId":"HPQuiz_20261017_PerugiaAqueduct"},"Ssd":"20261017","Name":"PerugiaAqueduct","Market":"ROW","Hash":"9255897123","FullDateString":"17 Oct 2026"},{"ImageContent":{"Description":"Some mountains make you work for the view. Mount Sneffels makes you work for the last few hundred metres. Rising to 4,310 metres in Colorado's San Juan Mountains, this fourteener is a familiar sight around Ridgway, Ouray and Telluride. It sits within the 67-square-kilometre Mount Sneffels Wilderness, a protected area designated in 1980. Hikers can reach the mountain from several approaches, including the Yankee Boy Basin and the Blue Lakes area.","Image":{"Url":"/th?id=OHR.SneffelsAutumn_ROW9027717849_1920x1080.webp","Wallpaper":"/th?id=OHR.SneffelsAutumn_ROW9027717849_1920x1200.jpg&rf=LaDigue_1920x1200.jpg","Downloadable":true},"Headline":"Info","Title":"Mount Sneffels in the San Juan Mountains near Ridgway, Colorado, United States","Copyright":"© lightphoto/Getty Images","SocialGood":null,"MapLink":{"Url":"","Link":""},"QuickFact":{"MainText":"","LinkUrl":"","LinkText":""},"TriviaUrl":"/search?q=Bing+homepage+quiz&filters=WQOskey:\"HPQuiz_20261016_SneffelsAutumn\"&FORM=HPQUIZ","BackstageUrl":"/search?q=Mount+Sneffels&form=hpcapt&filters=HpDate:\"20261016_0700\"+mgzv3configlist:\"BingQA_Encyclopedia_Layout\